---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import SocialRow from '../components/SocialRow.astro';
import { site } from '../site.config';
import { publications } from '../data/publications';
import { currently } from '../data/currently';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { person, webSite, profilePage } from '../lib/schema';

/** The About page shows the featured papers only; the rest stay in the data file. */
const recent = publications.filter((p) => p.featured);
---

<Page
  title="About"
  eyebrow="ABOUT"
  description={site.bio}
  schema={[profilePage(Astro.site, Astro.url), person(Astro.site), webSite(Astro.site)]}>
  <div class="lede-row">
    <div class="lede">
      <p>
        I'm {site.author}{site.authorCn && <> ({site.authorCn})</>}, based in Shanghai. I was most
        recently a Research Scientist at <a href="https://seed.bytedance.com/en/">ByteDance Seed</a>,
        on the AI for Science team led by <a href="https://web.cs.ucla.edu/~qgu/">Prof. Quanquan Gu</a
        >, where my work centered on generative modeling and cryo-electron microscopy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that, I worked on applied machine learning in <a href="https://www.bytedance.com/en/"
          >ByteDance</a
        >'s Education team, and on computer vision and medical imaging at <a
          href="https://www.sensetime.com/en">SenseTime</a
        >. I earned my M.S. from the <a href="https://en.ustc.edu.cn/"
          >University of Science and Technology of China</a
        > in 2019, working on image saliency detection.
      </p>
      <p>
        These days, I'm spending some time on mathematics, mostly as a way to step outside my usual
        toolkit and look at familiar problems from a different angle. I'm also using the time to
        read, experiment, and think more freely about intelligence and learning.
      </p>
    </div>
    {
      site.portrait && (
        <img class="portrait" src={url(site.portrait)} alt={site.author} width="132" height="132" />
      )
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Research Taste</h2>
  <p class="abstract" style="margin:0 0 14px">
    I have a bias toward taking things apart before adding anything new: what is actually broken,
    what is fundamental to the problem, what is just baggage from the current approach and what is
    the smallest change that would make a real difference? I like experiments where an idea has
    somewhere to die — small, controlled settings where you can tell why something worked, or why it
    didn't. If the signal survives there, then scale it. If it only appears after enough knobs,
    compute, and storytelling, I'm usually less interested. The best result, to me, is one that
    changes how I think the system should be built, not just a better number in a table.
  </p>
  <p class="research-interests">{site.interests}</p>

  <h2>Recent Research Work</h2>
  <div class="work">
    {
      recent.map((p) => (
        <div>
          <div class="work__head">
            <div class="work__title" set:html={p.title} />
            {p.links && (
              <div class="work__links">
                {p.links.map((l) => (
                  <a href={l.url}>{l.label}</a>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
          {p.desc && <div class="work__desc">{p.desc}</div>}
          <div class="mono-note" style="margin-top:4px">
            {p.venue} · {p.year}{p.coFirst && ' · co-first author'}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Currently</h2>
  <div class="stack stack--xs">
    {
      currently.map((row) => (
        <div class="kv">
          <span class="kv__key">{row.key}</span>
          <span>
            <span set:html={row.value} />
            {row.note && <span class="muted"> {row.note}</span>}
          </span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <hr class="rule rule--section" />

  <SocialRow />
</Page>
